WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.640
Kamil - Team Apart: Welcome, everyone, to the last talk of tonight.

2
00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:16.059
Kamil - Team Apart: at the AI Collusion Sprint, co-organized with Poseidon Research, A&E Studio, and sponsored by Model, AI Language Foundation, and Nebius.

3
00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:21.499
Kamil - Team Apart: For our last speaker tonight is Jerick Shi, a software engineer at the Volian Group in Berkeley.

4
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:30.160
Kamil - Team Apart: He graduated this year from Carnegie Mellon Master's in Computer Science, where he researched multi-agent LLM systems and AI safety.

5
00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:46.729
Kamil - Team Apart: Co-advised by Vincent Konitzer at CMU, and Shi Jing Jin at the University of Toronto. He is co-author of When Agents Lie, Premeditation, Persistent, and Exploitation in Repeated Games. Please welcome Jerick.

6
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.749
Jerick Shi: Wait, thank you, Kamil, for the intro, and…

7
00:00:52.760 --> 00:01:07.549
Jerick Shi: Yeah, without further ado, let's get started. Hopefully, the hackathon is going well for everyone. I think this is, like, what, the beginning of the hackathon, so… yeah, hope everyone is doing well, and yeah, my goal for this talk is just to give, like.

8
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:16.180
Jerick Shi: a bit more insight, or maybe some more ideas that people can work on based on, like, just some of, like, the different research themes that I have been working on, so…

9
00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:35.649
Jerick Shi: Yeah, as Kamil mentioned, I study at Carnegie Mellon, I focused on computer science, they were my masters, my research was mostly focused on multi-agent LLM systems and AI safety, where I was advised by Vincent Knitzer and Ching, and currently I work as a software engineer in the Bay, and I still work remotely with the Genesis Lab.

10
00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:49.649
Jerick Shi: So, going back to the overall context on why we're working on collusion, why we're, like, dealing with these sprints, things like that, we need to think back on, like, what happened with, like, AI safety in the past few years.

11
00:01:49.650 --> 00:02:05.660
Jerick Shi: So when we first thought about AI safety, we've been focusing a lot on single agent capability and safety. When you feed an LLM some sort of information, we want to make sure that the output is safe, it is not hallucinating, it doesn't give harmful information, etc, etc.

12
00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:28.549
Jerick Shi: But as everyone is starting to adopt these agents for themselves, there is a much bigger question we need to think about, which is this idea of a large group of LLM agents, because then there are a lot of new safety risks that we have never thought about before that needs to now be addressed, which is kind of why we're now thinking about, like, collusion in such a large scale.

13
00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:48.240
Jerick Shi: We're reaching a point where a lot of these different actions, a lot of these, like, regularly actions done by human are now done by these AI agents. So we now have AI agents that can negotiate with each other, they can set different prices in the markets, they can coordinate different tasks, so…

14
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:57.149
Jerick Shi: Yeah, because of that, there are a lot more things we need to think about, since these agents have essentially kind of taken over this world.

15
00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:22.540
Jerick Shi: there has been a lot, and I mean, like, a lot of work that has shown that LLMs currently kind of lie, they currently misrepresent all of its different actions. The earliest one goes all the way back to 2023. This author showed that if you feed GPT-40 some insider information regarding trading in the market, and then a manager asks it, like, what it knows about.

16
00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:46.109
Jerick Shi: It will purposely show in its reasoning that it hides this insider information. And that behavior, or similar behaviors, has then appeared many times in other different papers. They've shown that these LLM agents are capable of scheming, these agents are capable of colluding, algorithmic colluding, in fact, and I've kind of shown that these agents can then directly lie or break your promises.

17
00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:52.220
Jerick Shi: Even public to other people, other agents, when negotiating in these different scenarios.

18
00:03:53.020 --> 00:04:07.719
Jerick Shi: So, kind of going over the flow of what my research themes kind of look like, they all fall over this similar umbrella on how can we trust these LLM agents? Because…

19
00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:19.540
Jerick Shi: it's kind of hard to define trust or deception, and you can't just say whether an agent lies or doesn't lie. There is just a lot more nuance, and my goal is to kind of…

20
00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:25.900
Jerick Shi: Open up these new ones, dive deeper into these differences, and see exactly how we can, like, analyze these.

21
00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:27.890
Jerick Shi: deceptive behaviors.

22
00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:41.990
Jerick Shi: So just starting with one of my papers, essentially, the goal here is to show that deception isn't as easily defined as what people kind of thrown around. So…

23
00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:56.239
Jerick Shi: If we look into past few years' research, everyone has been saying that, oh, LLMs are becoming deceptive, they can harm other people, they can lie to each other, people have been just been throwing these words around, but it's hard

24
00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:08.230
Jerick Shi: It's hard to say exactly what do you mean by an agent trying to lie or deceive another agent or another person, because let's think about hallucination, for example.

25
00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:28.079
Jerick Shi: If we define deception purely as giving someone wrong information or convincing someone on a false fact, that's exactly what hallucination is. A hallucinated citation, for example. But if you kind of think about this from a much further perspective, that doesn't really feel like deception, because…

26
00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:42.380
Jerick Shi: A hallucinated citation feels more as a simple error, and LLM didn't intentionally feel like it's lying to achieve some other goal, so in that case, it's really hard to tie this concept of hallucination with deception.

27
00:05:42.820 --> 00:06:06.920
Jerick Shi: If we think about this a little further, what if an agent is told to, like, lie on command? Let's say you're playing a werewolf game with a bunch of LLM agents, and some of these agents are told that they are allowed to lie to deceive other players. This then becomes a lot more nuanced, because in these games, well, because these LLMs are prompted to then lie and deceive other agents, therefore they're considered deceptive.

28
00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:12.529
Jerick Shi: But again, is it really deception if they are essentially following instructions?

29
00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:18.689
Jerick Shi: So, in this paper, I claim that deception has to hit a few different categories.

30
00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:35.020
Jerick Shi: In addition, for purely saying that something is false, it also needs to hit the category that the model should have known better, aka, it still has the ability to produce a correct answer, and such a deceptive behavior has to serve some specific goal.

31
00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:58.509
Jerick Shi: So, in order to kind of, like, go a bit more technical, a bit more in-depth, these are kind of the three tiers I am proposing. Whenever we think about these different behaviors, we must first assume… we must first decide whether it is false or not. If it is false, that is immediately just misrepresentation, and this makes classifying things extremely easy, because most of these different papers

32
00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:04.520
Jerick Shi: fall into misrepresentation. I think this second tier, this Tier 1 deception, is…

33
00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:19.249
Jerick Shi: The hardest part to understand, but it's also the hardest part to classify, and is what a lot of the current research is going wrong, is this idea of divergence and functional intent. Divergence meaning it is still able to

34
00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:31.750
Jerick Shi: produce actions that are both right and wrong. And functional intent means that when it produces such a false information, it produces such an information because of some sort of underlying goal.

35
00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:45.269
Jerick Shi: And finally, we then add, like, a last tier, where if such a behavior then manipulates another person's choice, conditioned on the fact that it's already on this Tier 1 deception, then we call it manipulation.

36
00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:51.339
Jerick Shi: So, as a quick example, mask and insider trading are two

37
00:07:51.340 --> 00:08:16.290
Jerick Shi: pretty well-known benchmarks. I highly recommend people to take a look. Mask essentially shows that if you continuously pressure an LLM into, like, answering a question, you can continue pressuring it to give the wrong answer or the answer on the different side. So the question here is that, does this count as deception? Well, since the prompt doesn't say, like, give the incorrect answer or, like, order

38
00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:22.730
Jerick Shi: wrong answer, and since it starts off giving the right answer, that means that it does, in fact, hit divergence.

39
00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:34.980
Jerick Shi: But this LLM, because it was pressured to say the opposite answer, it was pressured to say the wrong answer, it doesn't have a purpose. It didn't have a real reason to kind of then give

40
00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:45.030
Jerick Shi: the wrong answer, essentially. So this is what this paper was missing, and why I would classify as purely as a T0 kind of paper.

41
00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:50.480
Jerick Shi: T0 doesn't mean bad, it just means that it only falls under the misrepresentation tier.

42
00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:57.749
Jerick Shi: Going back to insider trading paper… insider trading paper, where an agent can trade on some insider tip.

43
00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:10.469
Jerick Shi: Now, it has a purpose. It wants to hide this missing information so it does have functional intent. But it was never shown in the paper that it has the possibility, or it can choose.

44
00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:24.050
Jerick Shi: that it could actually act honestly without all of this different setup, which is why it doesn't hit this divergence criterion. So these are just a few examples on what it really takes to show that an LLM is pretty much deceptive.

45
00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:50.170
Jerick Shi: So just some overall takeaways, some overall thoughts from this paper, from what I have gotten, is that a lot of these papers, 92 of these different papers, have shown, or has claimed, in some way, in some sentence, that LLMs are becoming deceptive, they are showing deceptive behavior. I claim that that really isn't true, and what this means is that whenever

46
00:09:50.170 --> 00:10:02.940
Jerick Shi: people, or I guess, like, people in Hackathon, or in general, are thinking about, like, just collusion, or all of these different misrepresentative behaviors in general, think about exactly why, or how

47
00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:15.389
Jerick Shi: how exactly is it kind of manipulating? How exactly is it colluding? What evidence does it need to show that it is colluding? Or, in general, how to show that something is truly a deceptive behavior?

48
00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:27.429
Jerick Shi: Another flavor of my work that essentially goes into one of these different categories, one of these different deceptive behaviors, which I focus on promise breaking.

49
00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:50.349
Jerick Shi: So, the main idea here is that even breaking a promise itself is a bit nuanced, because there are a lot of scenarios where an LLM might be able to plan something beforehand, or announce something beforehand, and then actually do the action. There are a lot of combinations that you can think about under these different scenarios.

50
00:10:50.350 --> 00:11:05.010
Jerick Shi: So one example I like to bring up is Diner's Dilemma. It is a pretty famously known game theoretic setting. You have 5 different diners, they want to split the bill evening. Each of them have a choice to choose a cheap versus expensive dish.

51
00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:18.460
Jerick Shi: They all benefit if you choose the cheap dish, but then in the Nash Equilibrium, it benefits when one of them chooses an expensive dish, so they all choose the expensive dish. So imagine Prisoner's Dilemma, but in a multi-agent setting.

52
00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:23.240
Jerick Shi: Depending on what you do, you could first

53
00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:45.759
Jerick Shi: tell other agents that you'll choose the cheap dish, and then in the end, you secretly choose the expensive dish. This is something that I would call commitment-breaking, but if you essentially plan out first that you're gonna choose the expensive dish, you then tell other people that it's cheap, and then finally you order the expensive dish, this then goes into premeditated deception.

54
00:11:45.760 --> 00:12:03.369
Jerick Shi: And then this idea kind of follows when you go into these other different categories. Essentially, if you maybe initially, like, planned out that you're gonna be a good person, choose the cheap dish, but essentially, then in the end, switch your role and choose the expensive dish, that goes into impulsive deviation.

55
00:12:03.370 --> 00:12:19.950
Jerick Shi: So, the idea here is that then there are all these different kinds of deviations. Technically, they all count as deception in some sort of way, because you're going against what you said or what you planned earlier, but there are many of these different categories that have to be considered.

56
00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:46.069
Jerick Shi: This is kind of the overall experimental setup. Essentially, we focus on two different settings. In one of these different settings, it is a pretty simple example where every single one of these agents can choose what they want to say, and then finally, based on what everyone says, choose what action they actually want to do. This is kind of like the baseline scenario. We then go into a much more complicated scenario.

57
00:12:46.070 --> 00:13:01.180
Jerick Shi: where these agents can first plan, and then they announce, and then they do their private actions. So we add this planning step. And we can make this even more complicated if we repeat these games over several different rounds, then remembering what happened in these past different rounds.

58
00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:27.660
Jerick Shi: Perhaps one of the more… I wouldn't say interesting, it is an interesting finding, but a significant finding is that promise breaking doesn't depend either directly on the model or on the game. There has been a lot of terms thrown around where people say that maybe one LLM is more deceptive than the other, maybe one game produces, like, worse behaviors than the other. What I show is that it simply isn't true.

59
00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:43.149
Jerick Shi: You cannot find a single model that purely dominates another model, or a game that purely dominates another game. These LLMs are extremely finicky in the fact that depending on how you put these models and games together, you can get extremely different outcomes.

60
00:13:44.370 --> 00:14:04.200
Jerick Shi: Another thing that I want to show is that when you kind of measure these LLMs' reasonings, they often don't share that they're breaking promises in their reasonings, and that oftentimes, they don't really think about the fact that they're breaking promises, but more so they're just optimizing the solution.

61
00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:30.539
Jerick Shi: And finally, another thing I want to show kind of ties to the fact in finding one where, again, these LLMs are pretty interesting to work with, and the fact that these different models can have extremely different behaviors when you put them in a very same setting. You can have a model where, over time, they all converge to all of them going… saying that they're gonna do the cheap dish. You can go into scenarios where all agents will always go too into the expensive

62
00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:42.439
Jerick Shi: dish. You can have cases where it goes into round robin, where it's kind of like a tit-tat method. Sometimes they say cheap, sometimes they say expensive, and then go back and forth, which just makes… which just shows that it's…

63
00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:48.250
Jerick Shi: Shows a lot of interesting scenarios can happen when you mix these different models and games together.

64
00:14:49.430 --> 00:15:07.160
Jerick Shi: One final thing, this work is still kind of a work in progress, but this is kind of an idea that I like to call emergent deception, and I think this theme is slowly becoming much more relevant, I think, starting this year due to the increasing capabilities of LLMs.

65
00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:32.069
Jerick Shi: So, the main here, the main question I want to show is kind of addressing the fact in the first part, where a lot of people have shown that outlaws are deceptive purely because you tell them in a prompt that they can lie or manipulate other people. But what if you don't do that? Is it possible to achieve these same outcomes, achieve these same behaviors, purely due to environmental settings that are not easily as measurable in game theoretic setting?

66
00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:56.720
Jerick Shi: So this is just a toy simulation toy setting that I have been playing with. You have four different agents in a resource gathering game, you have gold, water, food, yadda yadda yadda, you can make trades, your goal is to make the most gold, but you want to keep this entire civilization alive with food and water. And the idea here is that over time, when you play this game, there is gonna be less fun

67
00:15:56.720 --> 00:16:11.939
Jerick Shi: food and water for everyone, but there's gonna be more and more gold, and because these agents, in their prompt, they like to have gold more than food or water, what agents will they plan on doing because of these environmental pressures?

68
00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:25.269
Jerick Shi: And the main thing I find is that these agents… okay, first of all, even without telling them at all that they should lie, or they should, like, kind of, like, dement me like other agents.

69
00:16:25.270 --> 00:16:50.209
Jerick Shi: The biggest finding I found is that they, in fact, are able to commit such actions without these different prompts. But in most of these different scenarios, the way they deceive other agents falls into the fact of selective disclosure. Essentially, they might say… they may try to hide some information that they found. Maybe they found, like, extra gold, they found extra food or water in a certain case, but they won't share it with the other agents just to hide

70
00:16:50.210 --> 00:16:55.990
Jerick Shi: for themselves. Sometimes they just act completely silent so they can act on this information themselves.

71
00:16:55.990 --> 00:17:14.410
Jerick Shi: These are some of the key, I guess, like, emergent deceptive properties that I have been seeing, and all of this has emerged without telling them that, okay, they should or they are allowed to commit these different actions. They explore it, they realized that these actions are, in fact, the most beneficial for themselves.

72
00:17:15.790 --> 00:17:28.050
Jerick Shi: So, from this, there are a lot of future directions, I would say, that can come from all of, kind of, what I've been working with. Some of them can then tie to this hackathon.

73
00:17:28.050 --> 00:17:52.890
Jerick Shi: So, one idea is essentially tying back to this insider trading information, right? The idea is that take all of these different games, or take all these different LLM agents, put them maybe in, like, some actual firm, and then have some monitor on them. Can you find some exact point, or can you find, under what pressure, do these agents kind of either go into silence.

74
00:17:52.890 --> 00:18:01.059
Jerick Shi: Or to kind of, like, hide hidden information, things like that. And this kind of goes into this idea of, like, maybe there's some, like, scaling off or deception.

75
00:18:01.070 --> 00:18:13.809
Jerick Shi: Another thing that I have been thinking about, which I think ties extremely closely to this hackathon, which I'll talk about later, are how these toy evaluations kind of connect to these real worlds.

76
00:18:13.870 --> 00:18:19.569
Jerick Shi: And I think this is a big gap, because if you look at some of the more recent papers.

77
00:18:19.670 --> 00:18:23.420
Jerick Shi: You realize that all of them go into…

78
00:18:23.480 --> 00:18:41.610
Jerick Shi: an extremely similar setting of, they kind of proposed this very specific outcome, they kind of proposed this very specific game, such as a werewolf game, such as a werewolf game with, like, some extra scenarios, without ever tying it to real-world cases, and I feel like this is an extremely big gap that needs to be solved.

79
00:18:41.930 --> 00:19:06.650
Jerick Shi: Some other things that I've been thinking about is essentially silence as a deceptive tactic in general. Obviously, in the case I just showed, when you're silent, misholding information, it kind of harms everyone but benefits yourself, but there's obviously certain cases, because of, like, privacy issues, that you do want to stay silent. So are there ways to kind of then increase this gap? And finally, I think the one that I am personally most interested in

80
00:19:06.650 --> 00:19:10.900
Jerick Shi: It's just scaling all of these different simulations.

81
00:19:10.900 --> 00:19:29.129
Jerick Shi: What happens when you maybe have 100 different agents under all of these different roles, maybe in, like, a bigger village civilization? What happens when you have one agent or one event that causes some sort of incident, and how that kind of, like, cascades over this entire civilization?

82
00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:53.200
Jerick Shi: And finally, I guess, like, as I kind of mentioned, by… from these future directions, this is how I personally feel like you could stand out in this hackathon. Every single talker's gonna have their own opinions. Some of the other talkers focus more on collusion, stenographic collusion, so they definitely have their, like, own takes, but this is personally just my opinions.

83
00:19:53.390 --> 00:20:09.140
Jerick Shi: I feel like there are two main ways, I think there are two main ideas that you can kind of pursue under these different, like, themes that were given. The first idea is kind of think about collusion in a more creative way. I think there are some…

84
00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:18.290
Jerick Shi: Most of collusion has been looked into it with the very same lens on how these different collusive behaviors can kind of emerge.

85
00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:43.259
Jerick Shi: My question is, can you think of some different way, some different way that collision can emerge, or some way to detect collusion that no one has ever thought of? And as you slowly do the literature research, you'll realize that a lot of them kind of fall into these different buckets. So if you can come up with something that's out of these different buckets, that would, in fact, stand out. That could be the environment itself, it could be exactly, like, what agent

86
00:20:43.260 --> 00:20:49.449
Jerick Shi: That you're playing with, or all of these different interactions or ways that these agents can share information to each other.

87
00:20:50.010 --> 00:20:52.030
Jerick Shi: The second way is…

88
00:20:52.140 --> 00:20:57.760
Jerick Shi: The second one is kind of what I mentioned a bit earlier, connecting these simulations to real-world risk.

89
00:20:57.770 --> 00:21:22.729
Jerick Shi: how do you… how do you convince people that, okay, these simulations are important, these simulations can happen on, like, some one-on-one correspondence with what might happen in the real world. And that just comes with thinking about, like, maybe other scaling up experiments, grounding scenarios, and what might have happened in the real world, and something that might be interesting to include is to maybe tell these different firms, like, maybe

90
00:21:22.730 --> 00:21:39.349
Jerick Shi: like, a very simple explanation on just one step where this one agent can, like, mitigate collusion or stop, like, these deceptive behaviors from happening. So these are just some of my food for thought on how to make the best out of this hackathon.

91
00:21:40.250 --> 00:21:59.170
Jerick Shi: But yeah, that is the end of my talk. I'm open to any questions, ideas. That's my email, that's my LinkedIn and my website, and… yeah, I think Kamil sent, like, some of my papers in the chat, so feel free to take a look at that.

92
