00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:18.689
Jerick Shi: Perfect, yeah. So, for my second paper, I've kind of shown that

106
00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:33.570
Jerick Shi: it really depends, okay? I have seen scenarios, I have seen models where, initially, they all choose the worst outcome, they choose, like, defect, if we go into Prisoner's Dilemma's term, but they all then kind of, like, converge to…

107
00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:58.359
Jerick Shi: what is the other side? Right, accepting, collaborating, whatever the other side is at the end. But I've also seen cases where they start the same way, they all, like, defect at the end. And then, I have seen a few models where, during the first 10 rounds, they kind of stay at some, like, 40-50% equilibrium, and then over 100 rounds, it kind of, like, goes to a 50-60% equilibrium. I personally think that's due to noise, I think that's still all around

108
00:23:58.360 --> 00:24:04.490
Jerick Shi: just, like, 50% equilibrium. But I think the answer here is… is that

109
00:24:04.490 --> 00:24:28.199
Jerick Shi: there really is no right answer. There has been a lot of proofs in this game theory setting that has shown that, like, if you play these, like, game theoretic games in general over, like, a repeated time, or, like, a lot of other scenarios, like, over these repeated settings, like, they will, like, converge to a specific equilibrium. I think it's called, like, Folk Theorem, which is interesting to look into.

110
00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:43.479
Jerick Shi: But I think because of how these LLMs are trained, and the fact that this is… we never play an infinite game, there's no… there really isn't this big notion of infinite in the real world. We never, like, hit these equilibriums, and these LLMs don't play

111
00:24:43.480 --> 00:25:00.680
Jerick Shi: play perfectly Nash equilibriumly, which is why then these differences can occur, which is why I think the main takeaway here is that it just depends, try things out, analyze the results, and see why these outcomes happen. Hopefully that answers your question.

